      "id": 1,
      "text": "Laurel was founded as a lumber town in 1882, named after mountain laurel bushes that grew on the site.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        12,
        26,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laurel%2C_Mississippi",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The New Orleans and Northeastern Railroad arrived in 1881, sparking rapid economic growth and turning Laurel into a bustling rail hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laurel%2C_Mississippi",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Laurel’s historic downtown is the largest intact collection of early 20th‑century architecture in Mississippi, listed on the National Register in 1987.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laurel%2C_Mississippi",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The city hosts Mississippi’s oldest art museum, the Lauren Rogers Museum of Art, founded by the Rogers family.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laurel%2C_Mississippi",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In 1968, Martin Luther King Jr. visited Laurel, marking a significant moment in the town’s civil rights history.",
